# ── PRISM v2 deploy configuration ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy to .env and fill in. NEVER commit a real .env (it's gitignored).

# RPC + signer are passed on the CLI, e.g.:
#   forge script script/Deploy.s.sol --rpc-url $RPC_URL --account <keystore> --broadcast --verify
RPC_URL=

# Also required to RUN THE TEST SUITE: 12 of the test suites fork mainnet and read this directly, so
# `forge test` reports 14 failures with "environment variable ETH_RPC_URL not found" if it is unset.
# It is the same endpoint as RPC_URL; both exist because the scripts take one on the CLI and the tests
# read the other from the environment.
ETH_RPC_URL=

# ── Airdrop (from merkle/generate.mjs on your snapshot) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# MERKLE_ROOT: the root printed by generate.mjs. MIGRATION_AMOUNT: the airdrop reserve in wei, which
# must EQUAL MERKLE_TOTAL exactly — that is, the `totalAmount (wei)` printed by generate.mjs. The deploy
# requires equality, not a lower bound: a larger reserve mints PRISM that no proof can ever reach, taken
# straight out of the tradable float, and there is no sweep. Set MIGRATION_AMOUNT=0 for a pure fair-launch
# (no airdrop; set MERKLE_ROOT=0x00..00 — the two must agree or the deploy refuses).
MERKLE_ROOT=
MIGRATION_AMOUNT=

# MERKLE_TOTAL: the `totalAmount (wei)` printed by generate.mjs. The deploy refuses unless
# MIGRATION_AMOUNT == this, exactly. It is what catches a wei/ether slip: `4455` would otherwise pass
# every other check as a 4,455-WEI reserve that no real allocation can ever claim against.
MERKLE_TOTAL=

# ── Pool seed (single-sided PRISM liquidity) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# READ DEPLOY.md SECTION 2 BEFORE FILLING THESE IN. Three of the four can deploy successfully and
# leave the token permanently broken; a revert is the safe outcome, not the dangerous one.
#
# SEED_TICK_UPPER is your LAUNCH PRICE. Derive it from your target FDV:
#     tickUpper = floor( ln(5000 / FDV_in_ETH) / ln(1.0001) / 200 ) * 200
# SEED_SQRT_PRICE_X96 must be the price AT that tick, not above it — anything above is a phantom quote
# with no liquidity behind it, which a one-wei trade erases for free:
#     sqrtPriceX96 = TickMath.getSqrtPriceAtTick(tickUpper)
# Take it from DEPLOY.md's table or from Uniswap's integer TickMath — NEVER from a spreadsheet. A
# floor(sqrt(1.0001^t) * 2^96) formula is wrong by construction: TickMath rounds the square root UP,
# so the float answer lands one wei low, the pool opens one tick below tickUpper, and seed() reverts
# MaximumAmountExceeded (0x31e30ad0). Note the v4-core vendored here is partial and has no TickMath.
# SEED_TICK_LOWER is your DEPTH; -887200 for a full curve. A narrow range lets one buyer take the float.
# SEED_LIQUIDITY sets how much PRISM actually enters the pool. PRISM per 1e18 of L depends ENTIRELY on
# the tick (it is L * 1.0001^(tickUpper/2)): ~46 at tick 76600, but 22.2 at 62000, 7.03 at 39000,
# 6.553 at 37600, 2.23 at 16000. Sizing from "46" under-seeds by up to 20x — see DEPLOY.md section 2.
# It must require <= (5000 - MIGRATION_AMOUNT/1e18) PRISM, and should require nearly all of it — the
# remainder is stranded in the hook forever.
# Ticks must be multiples of 200 and within +-887200. Every field here is silently narrowed
# (uint160/int24/uint128) if you add a digit; the script round-trips them, but count them yourself.
SEED_SQRT_PRICE_X96=
SEED_TICK_LOWER=
SEED_TICK_UPPER=
SEED_LIQUIDITY=

# The valuation you INTEND, in wei of ETH. The deploy refuses unless the price implies an FDV within
# 25% of this. Alignment and range say nothing about price: SEED_TICK_UPPER=887200 is aligned and
# in-range and sells the entire supply for one gwei, and the liquidity it needs looks like "272", so
# counting digits gives no warning.
#
# This only catches a wrong price if the number is arrived at INDEPENDENTLY of the price. Derive it from
# the tick and the band checks the price against itself — `impliedFdv(p)` within 25% of `impliedFdv(p)` —
# which is true for every tick, including a mistyped one. Alignment will not save you either: 4800,
# 14800, 48400 and 84400 are all multiples of 200. So state the valuation you mean from your own
# reckoning, or let `merkle/make-env.mjs` emit it — that script holds the valuation a second time as
# `LAUNCH_FDV_ETH` and refuses to write a .env when the two disagree.
TARGET_FDV_WEI=

# Minimum PRISM the seed must actually deposit. May only RAISE the bar — a value below 90% of the
# float is ignored, because as a downward override it was worse than no check at all.
# Guards an undersized seed: below one whole PRISM, totalShares can never leave zero, so pokeFees
# returns early forever and every fee the pool ever earns becomes permanently unclaimable.
#
# Set it to the seed you actually intend, NOT to 90% of the float. The floor is already 90% internally,
# so restating it leaves this knob inert and lets a mistakenly small SEED_LIQUIDITY strand up to 10% of
# the float — 54.53 PRISM at the shipped airdrop size — in a hook that earns no fee shares and, once
# ownership is renounced, can never release it.
MIN_SEED_PRISM=

# Optional, and the only seed check that runs ON CHAIN. `Renounce.s.sol` refuses to give up ownership
# unless the hook's position holds at least this much liquidity. Every other seed guard lives in
# `Deploy.s.sol`, which means it runs in SIMULATION only — so if a step reverts and you finish `seed()` by
# hand (a `cast send` with four typed values and no validation), this is the one thing standing between a
# mistyped liquidity and a permanently under-seeded pool in a contract about to become ownerless. `seed()`
# is one-shot, so that state cannot be retried.
#
# Both generators emit it as SEED_LIQUIDITY less one part in ten thousand. Leave it blank to skip the
# check. It must not exceed SEED_LIQUIDITY or the renounce step will refuse — Preflight catches that.
MIN_SEED_LIQUIDITY=

# REQUIRED. Path to the canary that merkle/generate.mjs writes — normally `merkle/out/canary.json`,
# relative to the repo root. The deploy reads it and re-verifies its (account, amount, proof) leaf
# against MERKLE_ROOT before anything is broadcast.
#
# This is the ONLY check that can catch a root which is perfectly well-formed but built from the wrong
# snapshot. Every other check compares one env value against another and so cannot see it, and nothing
# on-chain can either: PrismMigration has no sweep, so a wrong root locks the whole airdrop reserve
# forever. A stale root left in .env after regenerating the tree is the realistic way that happens.
#
# If foundry reports this variable missing, do NOT work around it by removing the check — run
# merkle/prepare-basis.mjs then merkle/generate.mjs, and point this at the canary.json they write.
# Pre-filled with the SHIPPED canary, which is what the runbook uses. `merkle/out/canary.json` is where
# generate.mjs writes when you rebuild the tree yourself; point it there only if you did.
CANARY_PATH=airdrop/canary.json

# REQUIRED, and it must be a random SECRET. The owner address and chain id are both public, so with a
# nonce of 0 the winning CREATE2 salt is fully derivable and the predicted address can be squatted on
# demand. Treat it like a key: never commit it, never share it, and
# pick a fresh one if a deploy attempt is ever griefed.
SALT_NONCE=
